window.docContents[193].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations--Main-objects":{"contents":"Finite-dimensional space is Mathlib's Euclidean type .\nSpacetime points carry a distinguished time coordinate and a spatial\nprojection. Coordinate derivatives are derived from Mathlib's Fréchet\nderivative, and the familiar operators are finite coordinate constructions\nbuilt on top of it:\n\n\n\nThe coordinate identity  holds\nunconditionally for these total Lean expressions. Interpreting them as\nclassical derivatives requires regularity. In particular, local \nregularity identifies this iterated-coordinate Laplacian with Mathlib's\nLaplacian defined through the second Fréchet derivative.\n\nA locally integrable function \nacts on compactly supported smooth test functions  by integration,\n, and distributional differentiation pushes the\nderivative onto the test function with the usual sign:\n\n\n\nDistributional differentiation also gives meaning to derivatives of\ndiscontinuous functions. The standard illustration is the Heaviside step\nfunction on . It has no derivative at the origin in any pointwise\nsense, and yet its distributional derivative is the Dirac mass ,\nbecause  for every test function\n. PDE theory needs this much generality. Solutions of hyperbolic\nconservation laws grow jumps like that in finite time, and elliptic theory has\nto cope with forcing terms that are rough to begin with.\n\nThe second Green identity on a ball\nrelates the interior equations of two  functions to their values and\nnormal derivatives on the sphere. Gauss–Green extends this calculation to\nbounded  graph domains, using intrinsic surface measure and outward normals.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Operators, solution concepts, and weak formulations","header":"3.1.2. Main objects","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations--Main-objects"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Characteristics___-Waves___-and-Nonlinear-Models--Conservation-laws-and-Hamilton___Jacobi-equations":{"contents":"For scalar one-dimensional conservation laws, weak solutions and entropy\nsolutions are kept distinct. The Burgers shock and rarefaction profiles\nsatisfy the positive-time weak and entropy identities and reach their\nRiemann data through a strong local initial trace. For a  flux,\nuniformly bounded entropy solutions with strong local  initial traces\nobey Kružkov's local  contraction, so two of them with the same trace\nagree almost everywhere. For a  flux with  bounded below by a\npositive constant, the Lax–Oleinik formula gives a weak solution for every\n datum and the sharp one-sided bound\n for  when .\nThis bound applies to the selected solution even when minimizers are not unique.\n\nFor smooth scalar data, the characteristic construction covers the entire\nspatial line before the first degeneracy time. The maximality theorem excludes\na longer classical strip without requiring a characteristic to attain that\ntime; gradient blow-up along a particular characteristic additionally assumes\nattainment. For a curved shock, two classical side fields satisfying\nRankine–Hugoniot and downward trace ordering give a whole-spacetime entropy\nsolution when the flux is convex between the traces.\n\nFor finite-dimensional systems we prove that the conservative and quasilinear\nresiduals agree, develop hyperbolicity and characteristic fields, record the\nelementary wave predicates, construct analytic local wave families from supplied\ncharacteristic flows, and prove the inverse-function stage of the local Riemann\nsolver. There is also a Bochner-valued interior weak formulation and a concrete\np-system entropy and Riemann-invariant chain. For the p-system specifically, the\nshock and rarefaction branches assemble into a local mechanically\nentropy-admissible Riemann solution with a strong local  initial trace.\nAn almost-everywhere limit of uniformly bounded classical viscous solutions\nis an entropy solution with the limiting initial datum.\nFor a general  strictly hyperbolic system whose families are each\ngenuinely nonlinear or linearly degenerate, the local Riemann problem with\nnearby states has a self-similar weak solution with a strong local \ninitial trace, glued from elementary connections between successive states\n(Evans, §11.2.4); entropy admissibility is proved for the p-system.\n\nFor a convex superlinear Hamiltonian on a finite-dimensional real inner\nproduct space and Lipschitz initial data, the Hopf–Lax value equals the\nminimum over absolutely continuous paths with finite action. A straight path\nfrom a minimizing footpoint attains it; see\n.\n\nFor one-dimensional Hamiltonians with finite Legendre transform and\nLipschitz initial data, the Hopf–Lax formula agrees with the absolutely\ncontinuous path formula. Its operators form a semigroup on nonnegative\ntimes, preserve order, and preserve uniform distance bounds. For convex\ndifferentiable , the formula also gives a continuous viscosity solution\non positive time with the initial trace; bounded Lipschitz data give a\nclosed-time initial-value solution. Tests need only be differentiable at\ncontact. Spatial dependence is allowed in the scalar viscosity definitions,\nwhile these constructed solutions use autonomous .\n\nOn a finite-dimensional real inner product space, a real-valued convex\nsuperlinear Lagrangian and bounded uniformly continuous initial data also\nconstruct a Hopf–Lax viscosity initial-value solution for the dual Hamiltonian.\nThe data need not be Lipschitz, and neither the Lagrangian nor its dual is\nassumed differentiable. This theorem uses locally  tests. Its solution is\nunique for nonnegative times among solutions bounded and uniformly continuous\non each finite closed time slab.\n\nComparison also holds for continuous autonomous Hamiltonians on any\nfinite-dimensional real inner product space, using tests that are locally\n. If the sub- and supersolution are bounded and uniformly continuous\non every finite closed time slab, initial ordering propagates to all\nnonnegative times, giving uniqueness in this class. Scalar solutions defined\nwith differentiable-contact tests also satisfy these local- tests after\nchanging from  to  coordinates; the same holds separately for\nsub- and supersolutions. For a position-dependent\n, uniformly Lipschitz in  and with\n, comparison and uniqueness hold in\nthe same slab class. Perron's method gives a continuous viscosity solution\nof the autonomous equation on the interior of a compact spacetime set,\nbetween continuous sub- and supersolution barriers that agree on its\nfrontier.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Characteristics, Waves, and Nonlinear Models","header":"7.6.1.2. Conservation laws and Hamilton–Jacobi equations","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Characteristics___-Waves___-and-Nonlinear-Models--Conservation-laws-and-Hamilton___Jacobi-equations"}});